 **Pitfalls**
 *   **Too Large:** A threshold that is too large causes dilation, leading to the loss of boundary details and large depth errors due to bounded rendering noise [S1].
-*   **Too Small:** A threshold that is too small significantly increases rendering time. If a maximum number of tracing steps is enforced, pixels may fail to converge, resulting in erosion (background misclassification) [S1].
-*   **Angle Dependence:** The threshold varies with $\theta$. Rays at larger angles (peripheral pixels) have a smaller $\epsilon$, requiring more precision [S1].
